    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "A museum has three integral goals – to entertain, support learning, and build dialogue\r\namongst its visitors and between visitors and the content on display (Simon, 2010).\r\nInformal learning experiences in settings such as museums, zoos, and aquariums can\r\nbe powerful drivers of affective and cognitive growth in learners young and old\r\n(DeWitt & Storksdieck, 2008). Specifically, grade school field trips are some of the\r\nmost memorable and exciting portions of a student’s educational career. However,\r\nwithout purposeful pre-visit preparation, students are not oriented to the physical\r\nspace of the museum prior to arrival and may not be able to access the concepts\r\npresented in the museum in a single visit (Hein, 1998). In Curativity, we present a new\r\nway to prepare students for learning through a shared project-based experience\r\nbefore a field trip excursion so that students see field trips, and museums in turn, as\r\nexperiences and places that are fun, educational, and accessible.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Colgan, Corinne and Radeva, Anastassia. Curativity - Exhibit Your Story . Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/kw833ky7745",
            "type": "preferred citation",
